## Throwing is wrong for collections

A resolver that returns *one* document should fail closed and throw. A resolver that returns *many* must not: throwing on the first forbidden document turns one protected item into a total failure for a caller who was entitled to the rest of the list. Collection resolvers **drop** what the caller cannot read and return the remainder.

The shape has two parts — a policy-wide short circuit, then a per-item non-throwing check:

```typescript
import type { CanonicalDocumentId } from "@powerhousedao/reactor-api";

searchTodos: async (
  _parent,
  args: { driveId: string; searchTerm: string },
  ctx: Context,
) => {
  const matches = await runSearch(args.driveId, args.searchTerm); // document ids

  // Policy-wide callers see everything; skip the per-item check.
  if (subgraph.authorizationService.isSupremeAdmin(ctx.user?.address)) {
    return matches;
  }

  const visible: string[] = [];
  for (const id of matches) {
    // non-throwing; ids returned by the Reactor are already canonical
    if (await subgraph.canReadDocument(id as CanonicalDocumentId, ctx)) {
      visible.push(id);
    }
  }
  return visible;
};
```

## Why filtering, not post-hoc shaping

Filtering **on read** means the resolver never leaks the *existence* of a protected document through a list endpoint. That is a stronger property than redacting fields after the fetch: an id in a search result is itself information, and a caller who can enumerate ids can probe them individually.

The `isSupremeAdmin` short circuit is a performance concession, not a correctness one — supreme admins and the `OPEN` policy would pass every per-item check anyway, so the loop is pure overhead for them.

This is the same shape the built-in `findDocuments` and relationship resolvers use. The corresponding best practice is stated plainly: filter lists, don't throw on them; reserve the throwing asserts for single-target resolvers.

